<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Genoese Gazaria</h1><p class="st-meta">1266 CE – 1475 CE (approx.) · capital Caffa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Genoa's string of trading colonies on the Crimean coast, centred on Caffa, a hub of the Black Sea slave, grain and silk trades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the restoration of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 1261 gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, Genoese merchants obtained the right to settle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a>, modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/theodosia">Feodosia</a>, from the khan of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> around 1266. Over the next century they added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudak">Soldaia</a>, now Sudak, in 1365 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaklava">Cembalo</a>, now Balaklava.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> was governed by a consul sent from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> and supervised by the Office of Gazaria there. Its merchants traded grain, fish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and especially slaves, many sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Egypt</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. Relations with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> were tense: during a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a> in 1346 plague broke out, and ships from Caffa may have helped carry it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1453 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> handed the colonies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-saint-george">Bank of San Giorgio</a>, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> controlled the straits. In 1475 an Ottoman fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedik-ahmed-pasha">Gedik Ahmed Pasha</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> and the other colonies, ending Italian rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/genoese-gazaria.jpg" alt="Genoese Gazaria" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Genoa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1266 CE – 1475 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caffa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ligurian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Genoese colonies in Crimea, Caffa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1370&amp;focus=genoese-gazaria" aria-label="Genoese Gazaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 587 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Genoese Gazaria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5842 843L5214 863M5842 843L6352 637M5842 843L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5214 863h0M6352 637h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5842 843h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5842 843h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1370&amp;focus=genoese-gazaria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

